Transform `.env` files into multiple output formats.

Supports 15 formats including JSON, YAML, cloud configs, CI/CD variables, and infrastructure-as-code. It writes a file or prints one; `evnx migrate` talks to a provider instead.

Usage: evnx convert [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --from <FORMAT>
          What the input is: `dotenv` (default) or `json`
          
          `json` reads a flat `{"KEY": "value"}` object, the same shape `validate --input-format json` reads. Every value must be a string.

          Possible values:
          - dotenv: A `.env` file
          - json:   A flat `{"KEY": "value"}` object
          
          [default: dotenv]

      --to <FORMAT>
          Target output format (omit for interactive selection)
          
          Supported: json, yaml, shell, dotenv, aws-secrets, gcp-secrets, azure-keyvault, github, docker-compose, kubernetes, terraform, doppler, heroku, vercel, railway
          
          [possible values: json, yaml, shell, dotenv, aws-secrets, gcp-secrets, azure-keyvault, github, docker-compose, kubernetes, terraform, doppler, heroku, vercel, railway]

  -o, --output <FILE>
          Write output to file instead of stdout

      --force
          Replace FILE when it already exists (with --output).
          
          Without it an existing output is refused and nothing is written. The input file is never replaced, even with --force.

      --gitignore
          Add the --output file to .gitignore without asking (no prompt).
          
          The file holds every value from the .env. Without this flag evnx asks when there is a terminal, and warns when there is not.

      --no-gitignore
          Skip the .gitignore check for the --output file

      --include <PATTERN>
          Include only variables whose name matches a glob pattern
          
          `*` matches any run of characters, `?` exactly one; case-sensitive. Repeat the flag or separate patterns with commas. Examples: "AWS_*", "*_URL", "DB_*_URL", "PORT,API_*"

      --exclude <PATTERN>
          Exclude variables whose name matches a glob pattern
          
          Same patterns as --include; exclude wins over include. Examples: "*_DEBUG", "TEST_*"

      --only <WHICH>
          Keep only the secrets, or only the rest
          
          A variable is a secret when its name says so (`*_PASSWORD`, `*_SECRET`, `*_TOKEN`, …) or its value does (a provider key, a private key, a URL with a password). Applied with --include and --exclude. For example, public values to a build system and secrets to a secret manager.

          Possible values:
          - secrets: Variables whose name or value says secret
          - public:  Everything else

      --allow-empty
          Convert even when no variable is left (an empty .env, or filters that match nothing). Without it that is an error, exit 2

      --allow-collisions
          When two names become one name in the output, keep the last value.
          
          `--transform` can merge names (`Api` and `API` under uppercase), and so can a format's own naming: gcp-secrets lowercases and uses `-`, and Azure Key Vault and GitHub ignore case. Without this flag that is an error naming both, exit 2.

      --base64
          Base64-encode all values before output.
          
          For `kubernetes` this chooses the field: with the flag values are base64 under `data:`; without it they are literal under `stringData:`, which Kubernetes encodes itself on apply.

      --prefix <PREFIX>
          Prefix to prepend to all variable names
          
          Example: --prefix "APP_" transforms "KEY" → "APP_KEY" Applied before key casing transformation
          
          Letters, digits and `_`, not starting with a digit, so every name stays a variable name.

      --transform <MODE>
          Transform variable name casing
          
          Options: uppercase, lowercase, camelCase, snake_case
          
          Examples: "database_url" --transform uppercase → "DATABASE_URL" "DATABASE_URL" --transform camelCase → "databaseUrl" "DatabaseURL" --transform snake_case → "database_url"

      --project <PROJECT>
          Google Cloud project for --to gcp-secrets (required there)

      --vault-name <NAME>
          Azure Key Vault for --to azure-keyvault (required there)

      --heroku-app <APP>
          Heroku app for --to heroku (required there)

      --name <NAME>
          Name of the Kubernetes Secret for --to kubernetes (required there)

      --namespace <NAMESPACE>
          Kubernetes namespace for --to kubernetes (optional)

      --kind <KIND>
          What --to kubernetes writes: `secret` (default), `configmap`, or `split`
          
          `configmap` writes every variable as plain text (not with --base64). `split` writes two documents: a Secret for the variables that look secret and a ConfigMap named `<name>-config` for the rest.

          Possible values:
          - secret:    One `Secret` holding every variable (the default)
          - configmap: One `ConfigMap` holding every variable, as plain text
          - split:     Two documents: a `Secret` for the variables that look secret and a `ConfigMap` (`<name>-config`) for the rest

      --terraform-declarations
          With --to terraform, write a .tf of `variable` blocks instead of the .tfvars values.
          
          A secret-looking variable gets `sensitive = true` and no default; supply its value from the .tfvars output or TF_VAR_<name>. Others keep their value as the default.

  -n, --dry-run
          With --output, check everything and say what would be written, but write nothing

      --no-color
          Disable colored output

  -q, --quiet
          Print nothing on success. Findings, warnings, errors and requested data still print

  -v, --verbose
          Enable verbose output

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Files:
      --env <FILE>
          Input file, or `-` to read standard input. Defaults to `.env`, or `[defaults] env_name` from `.evnx.toml`

      --env-name <NAME>
          Use `.env.<NAME>` instead of `.env`

Confirmation:
  -y, --yes
          Do not ask: add the --output file to .gitignore without the prompt (as `--gitignore`). With no `--to` it is an error naming `--to`, because the format menu is a question too.

Examples:
  # Basic JSON conversion
  evnx convert --to json

  # Kubernetes secret with transformations
  evnx convert /
    --to kubernetes --name myapp-env /
    --include "PROD_*" /
    --prefix "MYAPP_" /
    --transform uppercase /
    --output k8s-secret.yaml

  # A script that creates or updates every secret in a GCP project
  evnx convert --to gcp-secrets --project my-gcp-project -o upload.sh

  # A Kubernetes Secret for the secrets and a ConfigMap for the rest
  evnx convert --to kubernetes --name myapp --kind split -o k8s.yaml

  # A JSON file back into a .env, and a pipe in place of a file
  evnx convert --from json --to dotenv --env vars.json -o .env
  cat vars.json | evnx convert --from json --to dotenv --env -

  # Interactive mode (opens the format selector)
  evnx convert

convert writes a file; migrate talks to a provider.

Supported formats:
  Generic:        json, yaml, shell, dotenv
  Cloud:          aws-secrets (JSON for create-secret),
                  gcp-secrets (--project), azure-keyvault (--vault-name)
  CI/CD:          github (text to paste into the GitHub web form)
  Containers:     docker-compose, kubernetes (--name, optional --namespace)
  IaC:            terraform (.tfvars; --terraform-declarations for variable blocks)
  Secret Managers: doppler, heroku (--heroku-app), vercel,
                  railway (experimental)

Aliases:
  k8s → kubernetes, tf → terraform, yml → yaml, env → dotenv
  compose → docker-compose

Use 'evnx convert' without --to for interactive format selection.

Exit codes:
    0  Ran, nothing to report
    2  Could not run: usage, config, I/O, or refused before any change
  141  Output closed early (broken pipe)

Full guide: https://docs.evnx.dev/cli/commands/convert
